% ======================= 1. INTRODUCTION =======================
\section{Introduction}
Creating new, realistic images has traditionally required artists, photographers or
designers. Classical image editing tools can only modify images that already exist, and
they cannot produce entirely new pictures that look like they belong to a real collection.

Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), introduced by Goodfellow et al.\ in 2014, solve
this problem with a simple idea: two neural networks are trained against each other. The
\textbf{Generator} takes random noise and tries to produce a realistic image. The
\textbf{Discriminator} looks at an image and decides whether it is real (from the dataset)
or fake (made by the Generator). The Generator improves by trying to fool the Discriminator,
and the Discriminator improves by catching the fakes. This competition is called
\textit{adversarial training}.

In this project, a Deep Convolutional GAN (DCGAN), proposed by Radford et al.\ (2016), was
implemented in TensorFlow/Keras and trained on the Fashion-MNIST dataset to generate new
synthetic images of clothing items. The trained Generator was connected to an interactive
interface built with ipywidgets in a Google Colab notebook, where the user can choose a
saved training checkpoint, select the number of images, and generate new images with a
button click.

% ======================= 2. OBJECTIVES =======================
\section{Objectives}
The main objective of this project is to train a neural network that generates new
synthetic fashion images from random noise. The specific objectives are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item To design a DCGAN Generator that converts a 100-dimensional random noise vector
        into a $28\times28$ grayscale image using Dense, Reshape and transposed
        convolution (Conv2DTranspose) layers.
  \item To design a convolutional Discriminator that classifies images as real or fake.
  \item To implement a custom adversarial training loop using \texttt{tf.GradientTape},
        binary cross-entropy loss and separate Adam optimizers for the two networks.
  \item To record the training losses and save generated image grids and Generator
        checkpoints during training.
  \item To build a simple interactive interface that uses the trained Generator.
  \item To save the trained models in the native Keras format and reload the Generator
        to generate images without retraining.
  \item To study the quality and limitations of the generated images.
\end{enumerate}

% ======================= 3. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE =======================
\section{System Architecture}
The system has four main parts: the data pipeline, the Generator, the Discriminator and the
interactive interface. During training, the Generator creates fake images from random
noise, and the Discriminator receives real images from the dataset as well as these fake
images. The Discriminator output is used to compute two losses, one for each network. The
gradients of these losses are used by two separate Adam optimizers to update the weights of
the Generator and the Discriminator. The overall flow is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:arch}.

