\textbf{Interactive interface.} The interface, built with ipywidgets, loads the saved
Generator checkpoints. The user selects a checkpoint (epoch) from a dropdown, sets the
number of images with a slider, and clicks \emph{Generate new images}. A second button shows
the training loss graph, and a status message tells the user what the application is doing.
A Matplotlib-based fallback function is provided in case ipywidgets does not work.

% ======================= 4. DATASET =======================
\section{Dataset Used}
The Fashion-MNIST dataset, released by Zalando Research (Xiao, Rasul and Vollgraf, 2017),
was used. It contains 60,000 training images and 10,000 test images of clothing items.
Each image is a $28\times28$ grayscale picture, and there are 10 classes: T-shirt/top,
trouser, pullover, dress, coat, sandal, shirt, sneaker, bag and ankle boot. The dataset
is downloaded automatically through Keras, so no manual download is needed. It is small
and light, which makes it suitable for training a GAN quickly in a student environment.

A GAN learns the overall distribution of the images, so the class labels and the test set
were not used. Only the 60,000 training images were used.

\textbf{Preprocessing.} The pixel values were converted from the range $[0,255]$ to
$[-1,1]$ using $x' = (x-127.5)/127.5$. This matches the Tanh output of the Generator, so
real and generated images lie on the same scale. A channel dimension was added so that
each image has the shape $28\times28\times1$. The data was then shuffled and divided into
batches of \BatchSize\ images using \texttt{tf.data}.

