% ======================= 6. METHODOLOGY =======================
\section{Methodology}
\textbf{Setup.} The required libraries were imported, the availability of a GPU was
checked (training also runs on a CPU, but more slowly), and the random seeds of Python,
NumPy and TensorFlow were fixed to 42 for reproducible results.

\textbf{Data preparation.} The dataset was loaded, normalized to $[-1,1]$ and batched as
described in Section~4.

\textbf{Loss functions.} The Discriminator is trained to output 1 for real images and 0
for fake images. With binary cross-entropy (BCE), its loss is
\[
L_D = -\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[\log D(x)\right] - \mathbb{E}_{z}\left[\log\left(1 - D(G(z))\right)\right].
\]
The Generator is trained to make the Discriminator classify its images as real. The
commonly used non-saturating form of its loss is
\[
L_G = -\mathbb{E}_{z}\left[\log D(G(z))\right],
\]
which gives stronger gradients to the Generator early in training. In the code, BCE is used
with \texttt{from\_logits=True}, so the Sigmoid is applied inside the loss function, which is
numerically more stable.

\textbf{Training loop.} A custom training step, compiled with \texttt{tf.function}, was
written using \texttt{tf.GradientTape}. In each step, a batch of noise vectors is passed
through the Generator to create fake images. The Discriminator then scores the real batch
and the fake batch. The Discriminator loss (real images should score 1, fake images 0) and
the Generator loss (fake images should score 1) are computed, and two gradient tapes
give the gradients of each loss with respect to the weights of its own network. Two separate
Adam optimizers then update the Generator and the Discriminator.

\textbf{Monitoring and checkpoints.} The average Generator and Discriminator losses were
recorded at the end of every epoch. A fixed set of 16 noise vectors was used to generate and
save an image grid after every epoch, so the progress can be compared on the same inputs.
The Generator was also saved as a checkpoint after every epoch, which allows the interface
to generate images from any saved stage of training.

