\textbf{Discussion.} The results show that the Generator learned the general shapes of
clothing items from the dataset without using any labels. Because every checkpoint is saved,
the interface also lets the user compare the output of early and late stages of training.

Some limitations can also be seen. The model was trained for only \NumEpochs\ epochs, so the
images are recognizable but not realistic, and some images contain noisy textures or
unclear shapes. The Generator may also favour a few easy categories, such as shoes or bags,
and repeat similar images, which is called mode collapse. GAN training is unstable in
general, and the loss values alone do not show how good the images are. Finally, quality was
judged visually only, and the user cannot choose which type of clothing is generated.

% ======================= 9. CONCLUSION =======================
\section{Conclusion}
This project demonstrates the use of a Deep Convolutional GAN for image generation. A
Generator and a Discriminator were built in TensorFlow/Keras and trained against each other
on the Fashion-MNIST dataset, using binary cross-entropy loss, a custom \texttt{GradientTape}
training loop and separate Adam optimizers. The generated images improved during training,
starting from blurry blobs and developing recognizable clothing shapes. The trained Generator
was saved and reloaded to create new images without retraining, and an interactive interface
was built in which the user can choose a checkpoint and generate new images.

The main limitations are the short training time, the small image size, the lack of
control over the clothing type and the possibility of mode collapse. Despite these limits,
the project shows how two competing networks can learn to create new images from random
noise without any labels.

% ======================= 10. FUTURE SCOPE =======================
\section{Future Scope}
The project can be improved in several ways. Training for more epochs can produce sharper
and more varied images. A conditional GAN that uses the 10 class labels would let the user
choose the type of clothing to generate. More stable training methods such as the
Wasserstein GAN with gradient penalty (WGAN-GP) can reduce instability and mode collapse.
A quantitative metric such as the Fr\'echet Inception Distance (FID) can be added to
measure image quality. The same model can also be applied to larger and more complex
datasets at a higher resolution. Finally, the application can be deployed as a web
application so that it can be used from any device.

